  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...The nor'easter slowly departs the region through\ntonight.\n\nThe nor'easter is now centered south of Long Island, with high \npressure centered to the north. The low will continue to weaken \nas it tracks slowly northeastward.\n\nWinds have now weakened substantially compared to the past few\ndays, now only around 10 mph or so during the rest of the day \nout of the N to NW. Still some spotty sprinkles out there, but\ncoverage continues to mostly dwindle. Cloud coverage will \nlinger through tonight into Tuesday, though gradually waning\nthrough the period, with a few breaks possible this afternoon\nand much better chance of some sun on Tuesday. That said, not\nexpecting a wall-to-wall sunny day just yet.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Coastal flooding threat lowered in many areas but \nstill a concern along the back bays of New Jersey and Delaware.\n\nOverall, tidal flooding concerns continue to diminish as winds have \nshifted offshore. Additional flooding at high tides is expected to \nbe spotty minor for most locations. \n\nWith that being said, the Coastal Flood Advisory for Cape May, \nAtlantic, Monmouth, and Middlesex counties in NJ have been allowed \nto expire and additional coastal flooding headlines are not \nanticipated here. \n\nModerate flooding of back bays continues to affect Ocean County NJ \nand Sussex County DE. Given the offshore flow, water levels should \nbe receding from here. However, minor flooding is expected to \npersist through tonight and into Tuesday. A Coastal Flood Advisory \nis in effect until 2 PM Tuesday for these locations. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Unsettled weather possible Friday through\nSunday.\n\nA cold front is forecast to move across the area late Friday into \nFriday night. As it moves into the area, there will be a chance of \nshowers. However, as the vorticity impulse aloft weakens, the \nshowers may dissipate for many areas as the move eastward. However, \nadditional rain fall is expected to move back into the area Saturday \nnight into Sunday. An area of low pressure is forecast to develop \nalong the front as it stalls to our south Saturday, then move \nnorthward across the area through Sunday. Showers will increase \nSaturday night into Sunday as a couple of short wave/vorticity \nimpulses move into the area, along with the surface low\npressure.",
